Z||000002||286SE'S||08-30-2000||03:36 PM||||Their website is very interesting! lots of neat mods! but using my cheap calculator I convert $600 to about $445 American bucks. Too much!<BR>In order to have a completely factory sunroof the section of roof around the windshield all the way to the back (see the seam?) will be replaced with the sunroof version. That piece will have to be same as the cars color for the full-factory look. It may be part of the cost to paint this piece to YOUR cars color.<BR>Also the headliner will have to be cut and possibly recovered if it falls apart when it is removed. They are covering the cost of all this stuff I am sure.<P>Ask them how much if you supply the parts.<BR>Or consider doing it yourself. You may already need the new headliner.<P>This guy sells them for $120 with the storage parts and glass in your choice of colors!:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.usedfieroparts.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.usedfieroparts.com/</A> <P>By the way:<BR>The first Fiero I ever drove was in Niagara Falls. I drive from the Aquarium to the Tops market on portage/cedar. I had to walk back because the car wouldn't start. When I told my brother (his car) He said "yeah, it does that sometimes" It had a nice sunroof!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Master_Sushi||08-31-2000||05:54 PM||||Thanks 286se's. I think im not going to go and put the sunroof in. When I first got my fiero I really wanted to keep it stock, and having the roof cut up isnt very stock. Its only a 88 coupe, but I still think its to nice to keep non-stock. Also my headliner is in perfect shape as is most of the rest of the car. So I just cant see having one put in. There are allot more things to it then I was thinking of. I might jsut find a inexpensive fiero for winter driving (which I planed on doing anyway) and see if I can find one with a sunroof  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.<P>PS<BR>Ive been to the Tops market on portage/cedar quite a few times  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.||||reg||1
